capability crypto/md5  the MD5 digest of bytes or of text, broken for security and kept for compatibility
  heroes  crypto.md5, crypto.md5_text
  go      md5.Sum, md5.New, md5.Size, md5.BlockSize
  rust    -
  python  hashlib.md5
  note    RFC 1321's test suite answered as Python did; Go's `New` is `start(.md5)`

capability crypto/sha1  the SHA-1 digest of bytes or of text, broken for security and kept for compatibility
  heroes  crypto.sha1, crypto.sha1_text
  go      sha1.Sum, sha1.New, sha1.Size, sha1.BlockSize
  rust    -
  python  hashlib.sha1

capability crypto/sha256  the SHA-224 and SHA-256 digests of bytes or of text
  heroes  crypto.sha224, crypto.sha224_text, crypto.sha256, crypto.sha256_text
  go      sha256.Sum224, sha256.Sum256, sha256.New, sha256.New224, sha256.Size, sha256.Size224, sha256.BlockSize
  rust    -
  python  hashlib.sha224, hashlib.sha256
  note    FIPS 180-4's messages, a million `a` and a chain over every length from 0 to 300 answered as Python did; a megabyte and a PBKDF2 of 100000 iterations take 0.76 s together at -O2

capability crypto/sha512  the SHA-384, SHA-512, SHA-512/224 and SHA-512/256 digests of bytes or of text
  heroes  crypto.sha384, crypto.sha384_text, crypto.sha512, crypto.sha512_text, crypto.sha512_224, crypto.sha512_224_text, crypto.sha512_256, crypto.sha512_256_text
  go      sha512.Sum384, sha512.Sum512, sha512.Sum512_224, sha512.Sum512_256, sha512.New, sha512.New384, sha512.New512_224, sha512.New512_256, sha512.Size, sha512.Size224, sha512.Size256, sha512.Size384, sha512.BlockSize
  rust    -
  python  hashlib.sha384, hashlib.sha512
  note    Python reaches SHA-512/224 and SHA-512/256 through `hashlib.new`; their starting words are computed again by a test as FIPS 180-4 5.3.6 makes them, and Go answered SHA-512/224 of "abc" as Python did

capability crypto/algorithm  a digest named as a value, its sizes, and its name read and written
  heroes  crypto.Algorithm, crypto.digest_size, crypto.block_size, crypto.format_algorithm, crypto.parse_algorithm
  go      crypto.Hash, crypto.Hash.Size, crypto.Hash.String, crypto.Hash.HashFunc, crypto.Hash.New, crypto.Hash.Available
  rust    -
  python  hashlib.new, hashlib.algorithms_guaranteed, hashlib.algorithms_available
  note    the names are Python's, `sha512_256`; Go's `String` writes `SHA-512/256`, which `parse_algorithm` does not read, and Python's `new` also takes upper case. Every algorithm is always available here, so Go's `Available` and Python's two sets are the variant's cases

capability crypto/steps  a digest or an HMAC fed piece by piece and read at any moment
  heroes  crypto.start, crypto.update, crypto.update_text, crypto.digest
  go      hash.Hash.Write, hash.Hash.Sum, hash.Hash.Reset
  rust    -
  python  hashlib.hash.update, hashlib.hash.digest, hashlib.hash.digest_size, hashlib.hash.block_size, hashlib.hash.name
  note    a state is a value, so `digest` reads a copy and the state goes on, as Python's `digest` does; Go's `Reset` is a new `start`

capability crypto/copy  a state copied, to go on from one beginning in two ways
  heroes  built-in =
  go      sha3.SHA3.Clone
  rust    -
  python  hashlib.hash.copy, hmac.HMAC.copy

capability crypto/hex-digest  a digest written as hexadecimal
  heroes  bytes.format_hex
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  hashlib.hash.hexdigest, hmac.HMAC.hexdigest
  note    a digest is its bytes, and `bytes` writes bytes as text

capability crypto/hmac  the HMAC of a message under a key, at once or in steps
  heroes  crypto.hmac, crypto.hmac_text, crypto.start_hmac
  go      hmac.New
  rust    -
  python  hmac.new, hmac.digest, hmac.HMAC, hmac.HMAC.update, hmac.HMAC.digest, hmac.HMAC.digest_size, hmac.HMAC.block_size
  note    RFC 2202's and RFC 4231's cases answered as Python did, keys of a block's length and one more included; the outer key block is compressed once, so each tag costs one compression less than a plain HMAC

capability crypto/compare  two byte lists compared in a time their contents do not decide
  heroes  crypto.equals_constant_time
  go      subtle.ConstantTimeCompare, hmac.Equal
  rust    -
  python  hmac.compare_digest, secrets.compare_digest
  note    lists of different lengths answer false at once, as all three do; Python also compares two texts, which is `bytes.from_text` on both

capability crypto/hkdf  keys derived from a secret by HKDF, extracted, expanded, or both
  heroes  crypto.hkdf_extract, crypto.hkdf_expand, crypto.hkdf
  go      hkdf.Extract, hkdf.Expand, hkdf.Key
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    RFC 5869's cases answered as Go did; a length of 0 makes no bytes, as Go's does, and past 255 digests fails `invalid` where Go errs; `info` is bytes where Go's is a `string`

capability crypto/pbkdf2  a key derived from a password by PBKDF2
  heroes  crypto.pbkdf2
  go      pbkdf2.Key
  rust    -
  python  hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac
  note    RFC 6070's and RFC 7914's first cases answered as Python did; no iterations fail `invalid` as Python refuses them, where Go answered a key, since RFC 8018 asks a positive count; a length of 0 fails as both refuse it

