capability crypto/ciphers  encryption, decryption, signatures and key agreement
  heroes  refused: a cipher must compute in a time its key and its text do not decide, and nothing in this wave proves that of the C the compiler makes; a digest needs no such proof
  go      crypto.Signer, crypto.SignerOpts, crypto.SignMessage, crypto.MessageSigner, crypto.Decrypter, crypto.DecrypterOpts, crypto.Encapsulator, crypto.Decapsulator, crypto.PrivateKey, crypto.PublicKey, subtle.ConstantTimeByteEq, subtle.ConstantTimeEq, subtle.ConstantTimeLessOrEq, subtle.ConstantTimeSelect, subtle.ConstantTimeCopy, subtle.XORBytes, subtle.WithDataIndependentTiming
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Go's packages `crypto/aes`, `cipher`, `des`, `rc4`, `rsa`, `dsa`, `ecdsa`, `ed25519`, `ecdh`, `elliptic`, `mlkem`, `tls` and `x509` stand here whole; `subtle`'s selections serve the writing of such code. Refused for a cipher written in Heroes, and only for that: since 2026-11-17 `crypto/seal` (AES-128-GCM, AES-256-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305) and `crypto/keypairs` (Ed25519, ECDSA on P-256 and P-384, RSA-PSS and PKCS #1 v1.5 signatures, RSA-OAEP, X25519 and ECDH, keys as PKCS #8, SPKI, PEM and raw bytes) give these capabilities from the system's own cryptography: Linux's OpenSSL 3 offers them all; macOS offers neither sealing, nor Ed25519, nor X25519 to C (pending there, measured: CommonCrypto has no authenticated mode, `SecKey` no Curve25519 key, CryptoKit is Swift's alone); Windows' CNG has no Ed25519, and its X25519 is pending until the box measures its byte orders; the Windows arm is unrun (the box was off)

dropped registry  Go's table of hash constructors filled at start-up: there are no mutable globals, and `Algorithm` is closed
  go      crypto.RegisterHash
  rust    -
  python  -

dropped implementation-tables  the HMAC's padding tables, which `start_hmac` holds and nothing else needs
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  hmac.trans_36, hmac.trans_5C, hmac.digest_size, hmac.HMAC.name

dropped encoding-state  Go's saving of a running state to bytes and back; a state is a value, kept as long as the program keeps it
  go      sha3.SHA3.AppendBinary, sha3.SHA3.MarshalBinary, sha3.SHA3.UnmarshalBinary, sha3.SHAKE.AppendBinary, sha3.SHAKE.MarshalBinary, sha3.SHAKE.UnmarshalBinary
  rust    -
  python  -

dropped random  random bytes, tokens and choices are the package `random`'s
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  secrets.token_bytes, secrets.token_hex, secrets.token_urlsafe, secrets.choice, secrets.randbelow, secrets.randbits, secrets.SystemRandom, secrets.DEFAULT_ENTROPY, secrets.base64
